  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic of an ultrafast pump–probe optical setup. A single incoming beam at left is first dispersed by a grating and then split into two color-coded paths: a red pump pulse and a blue probe pulse. Both arms include “split-delay optics,” indicating that each pulse is divided into multiple time-delayed replicas, likely to create a controlled sequence of pulses before they reach the sample.\n\nThe red pump path is routed through reflective optics and a mirror, while the blue probe path follows a separate, longer route with its own delay elements. The two beams are then directed to overlap at the sample near the upper right. The geometry suggests the pump excites the sample and the probe interrogates the induced response after a tunable delay. After passing through or interacting with the sample, the blue probe continues to a detector, implying that the measurement is based on changes in the probe signal.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes timing control and beam routing: a broadband source is split, delayed, recombined at the sample, and the probe is measured to capture ultrafast dynamics.",
